privilege, a tradition later carried into George Henry Evansâ€™s land-reform movement. Broken off quill tip from David Dâ€™Angers's Thomas Jefferson statue (courtesy NYC Public Design Commission) In the early 20th century, trade unionist Eugene V. Debs cast socialism as a continuation of Americaâ€™s founding revolution. In 1912, he declared that the Socialist Party of America, which he helped found, was committed to â€œindustrial and social democracyâ€ and insisted that â€œeverything embraced in â€˜life, liberty, and the pursuit of happinessâ€™ is wrapped up in this issue.â€ Decades later in the 1970s and 80s, Michael Harrington, a founder and founding chair of the Democratic Socialists of America, drew on the tradition of â€œindustrial democracy,â€ associated with Debs and social activist and writer Upton Sinclair. This concept translated the republican ideal of independent and equal citizens, associated with Jeffersonâ€™s agrarian vision, into a modern economy by giving workers economic power and a voice over working conditions. In his theoretical writings, Harrington distinguished democratic socialism from Soviet communism and presented it as an extension of American republicanism. Mamdaniâ€™s rhetoric of â€œcollectivismâ€ needs this democratic scaffolding to gain national force. Reclaiming Jeffersonâ€™s language of equality in a continuation of the Mayorâ€™s Fourth of July address , and countering the neoliberal cultural elevation of elitist Alexander Hamilton, could furnish vocabulary and rhetorical weapons needed to confront oligarchic fascism. Defeating the threat requires more than policy arguments or denunciations of Donald Trump. It requires a public language presenting equality, worker power, pluralism, and resistance to oligarchy as expressions of the nationâ€™s deepest traditions. When Democracy Matters ends in November, Mamdani should return the sculpture from New York Historicalâ€™s ticketed galleries to a well-considered location in City Hall rather than permit another internal relocation. Mamdani could unveil it with a landmark address on democratic equality and the battle against racism and economic domination. The point would be not to absolve Jefferson, but to pick up his broken quill and recast his words for the 250th anniversary of the Declaration.ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;QzEjoIFr1DNaFJxvO3Pw8W7zngb6QudIaqLslKwBT7gƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx:Mayor Mamdani Should Reclaim the Cityâ€™s Jefferson Statueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;nQwskL2loSLlBxA4HFqCw8DQERPi2TB6a9F9ySyU7lcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkoSyndicationItemƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;wBcrVgO8iKKrqExmaZqFbpp0xq1SCvzkIwC_wCfLtW8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxRhttps://hyperallergic.com/mayor-mamdani-should-reclaim-the-citys-jefferson-statue/ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;AaYVuBkrN0irxR0EiCvIj6AKR56L07jTwZ40eI9bnW8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx8mayor-mamdani-should-reclaim-the-city-s-jefferson-statueƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;eZLeLQ4m6q1lWpOLgrp0fz4_YXeCf4cDJggrHHA161Uƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkwSyndicationSourceBundleƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;t7PajpVQxr0mVERbJnSCGjsFJy-yBw_9CRrfofxXLlQƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkgcultureƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jUyh75xQMYIMPMHDoJrcxPeJBROgsluMTdH-cB1_T2Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;s8d80x4Av7NfI5BiiJrLNE5pLsux3K9MOj1Luf0iuLYƒdpai